# [[ name ]]

A Terraform module for **[[ provider ]]**, scaffolded by
`ansari new --type terraform-module`. It declares its provider requirements and
leaves provider configuration to the caller.

## Usage

```hcl
module "[[ name | replace('-', '_') ]]" {
  source = "git::https://github.com/your-org/[[ name ]].git?ref=v0.1.0"

  name = "[[ name ]]"
}
```

## Inputs

| Name | Type | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | `string` | required | Name for everything this module creates: 2–63 lowercase letters, digits, or hyphens, starting with a letter. |
| `[[ kind ]]` | `map(string)` | `{}` | [[ kind | capitalize ]] applied to every resource this module creates. |

## Outputs

| Name | Description |
|---|---|
| `name` | The name this module was configured with. |
| `[[ kind ]]` | The [[ kind ]] this module applies to its resources. |

## Layout

- `versions.tf` — Terraform and provider version constraints
- `main.tf`, `variables.tf`, `outputs.tf` — the module
- `examples/basic/` — a root configuration that uses the module
- `.github/workflows/ansari.yml` — format and validation checks

## CI

`terraform fmt -check`, then `terraform init -backend=false` and
`terraform validate` for both the module and the example. None of these need
cloud credentials.

A `plan` job is included **commented out**. A module can't be planned without a
backend and real provider credentials; enable it only once this repository has
them.

## Staying current

This repository carries `.ansari/manifest.yaml`. `ansari check` reports when the
template it came from has moved on (a new provider major version, for example)
and which generated files have been edited here.
